WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Krystian Aparta

00:00:13.397 --> 00:00:16.119
So our story started several years ago,

00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:18.635
when my wife and I
got a complaint letter in the mail

00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:20.698
from an anonymous neighbor.

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:23.131
(Laughter)

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:27.839
I'll never forget the way
my wife transformed before my eyes

00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:31.603
from this graceful, peaceful, sweet woman

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:36.564
into just an angry mother grizzly bear
whose cubs needed to be protected.

00:00:36.588 --> 00:00:38.175
It was intense.

00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:40.342
So here's what happened.

00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:41.656
This is our family.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.566
This is my wife and I
and our five awesome kids.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.854
We're pretty loud,
we're pretty rambunctious,

00:00:47.878 --> 00:00:49.028
we're us.

00:00:49.657 --> 00:00:51.876
You'll notice, though,
that two of our children

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.871
look a little different than Mary and I,

00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:56.346
and that's because they came to us
through adoption.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.005
Our neighbor, though,
saw two different-looking children

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:01.640
playing outside of our house every day

00:01:01.664 --> 00:01:02.919
and came to the conclusion

00:01:02.943 --> 00:01:06.180
that we must have been running
an illegal day care out of our home.

00:01:06.204 --> 00:01:08.783
(Murmuring)

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.363
We were really angry to have
our children stereotyped like that,

00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:17.767
but I know that's a relatively
minor example of racial profiling.

00:01:18.934 --> 00:01:21.673
But isn't it sometimes
what we all tend to do

00:01:21.697 --> 00:01:24.006
with people who think differently,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.831
or believe differently
or maybe even vote differently?

00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:31.014
Instead of engaging as true neighbors,

00:01:31.038 --> 00:01:32.791
we keep our distance

00:01:32.815 --> 00:01:34.387
and our actions towards those

00:01:34.411 --> 00:01:38.077
are guided by who we think
sees the world as we do

00:01:38.101 --> 00:01:39.701
or who we think doesn't.

00:01:40.872 --> 00:01:44.785
See, what my neighbor suffered from
is a condition called agonism.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:48.849
And sometimes we all suffer
from the same condition.

00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:52.632
It's not a medical condition,
but it is contagious.

00:01:53.522 --> 00:01:55.926
So let's talk a little bit
about what agonism is.

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:58.404
My favorite definition of agonism

00:01:58.428 --> 00:02:04.187
is taking a warlike stance
in contexts that are not literally war.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:08.021
Agonism comes from the same
Greek root word "agon"

00:02:08.045 --> 00:02:09.494
from which we get "agony."

00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:11.370
How very appropriate.

00:02:13.649 --> 00:02:15.744
We all tend to show symptoms of agonism

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:18.321
when we hold on
to two deeply held beliefs,

00:02:18.345 --> 00:02:21.541
first identified by author Rick Warren.

00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:25.832
The first one is that if love someone,

00:02:25.856 --> 00:02:28.887
we must agree with all they do or believe.

00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:31.698
And the second is the inverse,

00:02:31.722 --> 00:02:33.316
that if we disagree with someone,

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.525
it must mean that we fear or we hate them.

00:02:37.811 --> 00:02:41.685
Not sure we really recognize the agony
this way of thinking brings to us,

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:44.089
when our relationships die

00:02:44.113 --> 00:02:47.109
because we think
we have to agree or disagree

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.878
no matter what.

00:02:49.807 --> 00:02:53.403
Think about the conversations
we've had around Brexit,

00:02:53.427 --> 00:02:55.126
or Hong Kong,

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.435
maybe Israeli settlements
or perhaps impeachment.

00:02:59.649 --> 00:03:02.763
I bet we could all think
of at least one personal relationship

00:03:02.787 --> 00:03:07.514
that's been strained or maybe even ended
because of these topics,

00:03:07.538 --> 00:03:08.887
or tragically,

00:03:08.911 --> 00:03:12.109
over a topic much more trivial than those.

00:03:13.276 --> 00:03:15.895
The cure for agonism is not out of reach.

00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:17.783
The question is how.

00:03:18.704 --> 00:03:20.664
So might I suggest two strategies

00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:23.307
that my experience
has taught me to start with.

00:03:24.141 --> 00:03:28.410
First, cultivate common ground,

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.967
which means focusing on what we share.

00:03:31.862 --> 00:03:35.149
I want you to know I'm using my words
very, very deliberately.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:38.377
By "cultivate," I mean
we have to intentionally work

00:03:38.401 --> 00:03:40.734
to find common ground with someone.

00:03:41.218 --> 00:03:44.035
Just like a farmer works
to cultivate the soil.

00:03:44.687 --> 00:03:47.252
And common ground is a common term,

00:03:47.276 --> 00:03:49.379
so let me at least explain
what I don't mean,

00:03:49.403 --> 00:03:52.847
which is I don't mean by common ground
that we were exact,

00:03:52.871 --> 00:03:55.037
or that we totally agree and approve.

00:03:55.061 --> 00:03:58.371
All I mean is that we find
one unifying thing

00:03:58.395 --> 00:04:03.307
that we can have in a relationship
in common with another person.

00:04:04.456 --> 00:04:07.098
You know, sometimes
that one thing is hard to find.

00:04:07.122 --> 00:04:08.956
So I'd like to share a personal story,

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.138
but before I do,

00:04:10.162 --> 00:04:12.400
let me tell you a little bit
more about myself.

00:04:12.424 --> 00:04:13.617
I'm Caucasian,

00:04:13.641 --> 00:04:16.599
cisgender male,

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:19.196
middle class, evangelical Christian.

00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:23.276
And I know, as soon as some
of those words came out of my mouth,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.395
some of you had some perceptions about me.

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:26.617
And it's OK,

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:29.402
I know that not all
those perceptions are positive.

00:04:30.411 --> 00:04:32.791
But for those who share my faith,

00:04:32.815 --> 00:04:35.712
know that I'm about
to cut across the grain.

00:04:35.736 --> 00:04:37.869
And you may tune me out as well.

00:04:38.315 --> 00:04:39.466
So as we go,

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.531
if you're having a hard time hearing me,

00:04:41.555 --> 00:04:44.156
I just gently ask that you reflect

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.823
and see if you're buying into agonism.

00:04:46.847 --> 00:04:48.045
If you're rejecting me

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:51.863
simply because you think
you see the world differently than I do,

00:04:51.887 --> 00:04:54.196
because isn't that
what we're here talking about?

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:55.890
Alright, ready?

00:04:56.398 --> 00:05:00.089
So I've been thinking a lot
about how to find common ground

00:05:00.113 --> 00:05:02.676
in the area of gender fluidity,

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.567
as an evangelical Christian.

00:05:05.427 --> 00:05:07.371
For Christians like me,

00:05:07.395 --> 00:05:11.323
we believe that God
created us man and woman.

00:05:11.553 --> 00:05:12.704
So what do I do?

00:05:12.728 --> 00:05:14.243
Do I throw up my hands and say,

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:17.094
"I can't have a relationship
with anybody who is transgender

00:05:17.118 --> 00:05:20.005
or LGBTQIA?"

00:05:20.029 --> 00:05:21.180
No.

00:05:21.204 --> 00:05:23.471
That would be giving into agonism.

00:05:24.164 --> 00:05:27.228
So I started looking
at the foundational aspects of my faith,

00:05:27.252 --> 00:05:28.965
the first of which

00:05:28.989 --> 00:05:33.021
is that of the three billion genes
that make us human --

00:05:33.045 --> 00:05:37.966
and by the way, we share
99.9 percent of those genes --

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:41.926
that I believe those three billion genes
are the result of an intelligent designer.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:45.672
And that immediately gives me
common ground with anybody.

00:05:45.696 --> 00:05:47.231
What it also gives me ...

00:05:49.454 --> 00:05:52.326
is the belief that each
and every one of us

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.398
have been given the right to life

00:05:55.422 --> 00:05:58.318
by that same intelligent designer.

00:06:02.514 --> 00:06:03.847
I dug deeper though.

00:06:04.752 --> 00:06:06.736
I found that my faith didn't teach me

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.054
to start relationships
by arguing with somebody

00:06:09.078 --> 00:06:11.624
until they believed what I believed,

00:06:11.648 --> 00:06:12.879
or I convinced them.

00:06:12.903 --> 00:06:15.073
No, it taught me to start relationships

00:06:15.097 --> 00:06:19.029
by loving them as a coequal member
of the human race.

00:06:21.357 --> 00:06:22.508
Honestly though,

00:06:22.532 --> 00:06:24.944
some who share my faith draw a line

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:28.809
and refuse to address somebody
by their preferred gender pronoun.

00:06:29.112 --> 00:06:32.561
But isn't that believing the lie
that in order for me to honor you,

00:06:32.585 --> 00:06:34.785
I have to give up what I believe?

00:06:37.616 --> 00:06:38.958
Come back in time with me --

00:06:38.982 --> 00:06:40.323
let's say it's 20 years ago,

00:06:40.347 --> 00:06:43.030
and Muhammad Ali comes to your doorstep.

00:06:43.054 --> 00:06:44.454
And you open the door.

00:06:45.078 --> 00:06:47.633
Would you address him as Muhammad Ali

00:06:47.657 --> 00:06:49.924
or his former name of Cassius Clay?

00:06:51.013 --> 00:06:54.347
I'm guessing that most of you
would say Muhammad Ali.

00:06:54.371 --> 00:06:56.799
And I'm also guessing that most of you

00:06:56.823 --> 00:07:00.521
wouldn't think we'd have to
immediately convert to Islam,

00:07:00.545 --> 00:07:02.079
just by using his name.

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:07.093
To honor him would cost me,
would cost any of us

00:07:07.117 --> 00:07:08.624
absolutely nothing,

00:07:08.648 --> 00:07:12.815
and it would give us the common ground
to have a relationship.

00:07:13.863 --> 00:07:16.814
And it's the relationship
that cures agonism,

00:07:16.838 --> 00:07:19.196
not giving up what we believe.

00:07:21.045 --> 00:07:23.728
So for me to honor my faith,

00:07:23.752 --> 00:07:26.997
it means rejecting
these rigid symptoms of agonism.

00:07:27.021 --> 00:07:29.894
Meaning, I can and I will love you.

00:07:29.918 --> 00:07:31.910
I can and I will accept you,

00:07:31.934 --> 00:07:35.243
and I don't have to buy into the lie

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:38.029
that if I do these things,
I have to give up what I believe

00:07:38.053 --> 00:07:40.148
or chose to fear and hate you.

00:07:40.617 --> 00:07:43.632
Because I'm focusing
on what we have in common.

00:07:47.045 --> 00:07:51.497
When you can find even the smallest bit
of common ground with somebody,

00:07:51.521 --> 00:07:56.765
it allows you to understand
just the beautiful wonder

00:07:56.789 --> 00:07:58.023
and complexity

00:07:59.415 --> 00:08:01.773
and majesty of the other person.

00:08:05.583 --> 00:08:07.579
Our second strategy

00:08:07.603 --> 00:08:09.855
gives us room to (Inhales)

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:11.029
breathe.

00:08:11.672 --> 00:08:12.822
To pause.

00:08:13.299 --> 00:08:14.751
To calm down.

00:08:15.474 --> 00:08:19.465
To have the kind of relationships
that cure agonism.

00:08:20.188 --> 00:08:22.988
And how to keep those relationships alive.

00:08:24.815 --> 00:08:30.238
Our second strategy is to exchange
extravagant grace.

00:08:30.262 --> 00:08:31.412
(Laughs)

00:08:32.101 --> 00:08:33.816
Once again, I'm not mincing words --

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.635
by grace, I don't mean
we should all go sign up for ballet,

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:37.899
that would be weird.

00:08:37.923 --> 00:08:39.204
(Laughter)

00:08:39.228 --> 00:08:43.561
What I mean is not canceling
everything over one mistake.

00:08:44.003 --> 00:08:46.446
Even if that mistake
personally offended you.

00:08:47.012 --> 00:08:48.212
Maybe even deeply.

00:08:49.703 --> 00:08:54.910
Perhaps Holocaust survivor
Corrie ten Boom put it best

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.489
when she said,

00:08:56.513 --> 00:09:00.593
"To forgive is to set a prisoner free,

00:09:00.617 --> 00:09:02.698
only to realize that prisoner was me."

00:09:06.009 --> 00:09:09.556
My faith teaches me that we humans
will never be perfect,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.313
myself very much included.

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:14.434
So we need the grace of a savior,

00:09:14.458 --> 00:09:16.330
who for me is Jesus.

00:09:17.275 --> 00:09:19.815
And while I define grace
in the context of my faith,

00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:24.013
I know there's a lot of other people
who have defined it differently

00:09:24.037 --> 00:09:25.299
and in different ways.

00:09:25.323 --> 00:09:28.923
One of my favorites is radio broadcaster
Oswald Hoffmann, who said,

00:09:28.947 --> 00:09:32.500
"Grace is the love that loves the unlovely

00:09:32.524 --> 00:09:34.587
and the unlovable."

00:09:35.064 --> 00:09:37.597
And I just love that picture of grace.

00:09:38.361 --> 00:09:39.551
Because I know I am,

00:09:39.575 --> 00:09:41.607
and maybe a lot of you can think of a time

00:09:41.631 --> 00:09:44.440
when we're just pretty dadgum unlovable.

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:52.450
So it would be the height of hypocrisy,

00:09:52.474 --> 00:09:55.751
dare I say repulsive to my faith,

00:09:55.775 --> 00:09:57.545
for me to accept

00:09:57.569 --> 00:10:01.958
the unconditional, unqualified
grace and love from God

00:10:01.982 --> 00:10:03.854
and then turn around

00:10:03.878 --> 00:10:07.759
and put one precondition
on the love I give you.

00:10:09.013 --> 00:10:11.546
What in the world would I be thinking?

00:10:16.886 --> 00:10:20.101
And by extravagant, I mean over the top,

00:10:20.125 --> 00:10:21.838
not just checking a box.

00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:24.402
We can all remember when we were kids

00:10:24.426 --> 00:10:26.796
and our parents forced us
to apologize to somebody

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.549
and we walked up to them
and said, (Angrily) "I'm sorry."

00:10:29.573 --> 00:10:31.235
We just got it over with, right?

00:10:31.259 --> 00:10:32.995
That's not what we're talking about.

00:10:33.019 --> 00:10:35.878
What we're talking about
is not having to give someone grace

00:10:35.902 --> 00:10:38.243
but choosing to and wanting to.

00:10:38.267 --> 00:10:41.592
That's how we exchange extravagant grace.

00:10:43.585 --> 00:10:46.712
Listen, I know this can sound
really, really theoretical.

00:10:46.736 --> 00:10:49.521
So I'd like to tell you
about a hero of mine.

00:10:49.847 --> 00:10:51.576
A hero of grace.

00:10:52.347 --> 00:10:54.235
It's 2014.

00:10:54.918 --> 00:10:56.068
In Iran.

00:10:56.696 --> 00:11:00.600
And the mother of a murdered son
is in a public square.

00:11:03.281 --> 00:11:06.844
The man who murdered her son
is also in that square,

00:11:06.868 --> 00:11:08.614
by a gallows,

00:11:09.313 --> 00:11:11.606
on a chair of some kind,

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.982
a noose around his neck

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:16.243
and a blindfold over his eyes.

00:11:17.736 --> 00:11:19.207
Samereh Alinejad

00:11:19.231 --> 00:11:23.244
had been given the sole right
under the laws of her country

00:11:23.268 --> 00:11:26.133
to either pardon this man

00:11:26.157 --> 00:11:28.323
or initiate his execution.

00:11:29.188 --> 00:11:31.648
Put another way, she could pardon him

00:11:31.672 --> 00:11:35.216
or literally push that chair
out from underneath his feet.

00:11:37.521 --> 00:11:38.671
(Exhales)

00:11:39.926 --> 00:11:41.076
I just ...

00:11:41.633 --> 00:11:45.562
I can't picture the agony
going through both Samereh and this man

00:11:45.586 --> 00:11:46.744
at the time.

00:11:46.768 --> 00:11:48.418
Samereh with her choice to make,

00:11:48.442 --> 00:11:52.069
and this man, in the account
that I read, was just weeping,

00:11:52.093 --> 00:11:54.489
just begging for forgiveness.

00:11:55.696 --> 00:11:57.296
And Samereh had a choice.

00:11:57.759 --> 00:12:00.616
And she chose in that moment
to walk up to this man

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.794
and to slap him right across the face.

00:12:03.818 --> 00:12:06.603
And that signaled her pardon.

00:12:08.707 --> 00:12:09.857
It gets better.

00:12:10.551 --> 00:12:13.738
Right afterwards, somebody asked her,

00:12:13.762 --> 00:12:16.269
they interviewed her,
and she was quoted as saying,

00:12:16.293 --> 00:12:20.070
"I felt as if rage vanished
from within my heart

00:12:20.094 --> 00:12:23.852
and the blood in my veins
began to flow again."

00:12:24.173 --> 00:12:25.640
Isn't that incredible?

00:12:26.021 --> 00:12:29.916
I mean, what a picture of grace,
what a hero of grace.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:32.193
And there's a lesson in there
for all of us.

00:12:32.217 --> 00:12:33.948
That as theologian John Piper said,

00:12:33.972 --> 00:12:38.864
"Grace is power, not just pardon."

00:12:39.974 --> 00:12:42.228
And if you think about it,

00:12:42.252 --> 00:12:45.580
grace is the gift we give
someone else in a relationship

00:12:45.604 --> 00:12:50.093
that says our relationship
is way more important

00:12:50.117 --> 00:12:51.902
than the things that separate us.

00:12:53.672 --> 00:12:56.172
And if you really think
about it some more,

00:12:56.196 --> 00:13:01.450
we all have the power to execute
in our relationships,

00:13:01.474 --> 00:13:02.624
or to pardon.

00:13:07.109 --> 00:13:10.389
We never did find out
the identity of our anonymous neighbor.

00:13:11.086 --> 00:13:12.236
(Laughter)

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.627
But if we did, I'd hope we'd simply say,

00:13:15.651 --> 00:13:17.051
"Can we have coffee?"

00:13:18.222 --> 00:13:20.806
And maybe there's somebody
you need to have coffee with

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:23.297
and find your common ground with them.

00:13:23.909 --> 00:13:26.829
Or maybe there's somebody
you're in a relationship with

00:13:26.853 --> 00:13:29.787
and you need to exchange
extravagant grace.

00:13:30.569 --> 00:13:31.719
Maybe go first.

00:13:33.323 --> 00:13:35.314
These two strategies have taught me

00:13:35.338 --> 00:13:38.574
how to exchange extravagant grace
in my relationships

00:13:38.598 --> 00:13:42.859
and to enjoy the beautiful design
of my neighbors.

00:13:44.169 --> 00:13:48.526
I want to continue to choose
relationships over agonism.

00:13:49.476 --> 00:13:51.276
Will you choose to join me?

00:13:52.174 --> 00:13:53.333
Thank you.

00:13:53.357 --> 00:13:58.864
(Applause)

